PART 1 — Create S3 Bucket + Upload File + Enable Public Access
What is S3?

Amazon S3 = Simple Storage Service.

Think of it like:

Google Drive inside AWS
Bucket = Folder
Object = File
STEP 1 — Login to AWS
Open:
Browser → Chrome
Go to:
AWS Console

Type in Google:

AWS Console login

OR open:

https://console.aws.amazon.com
Login using:
AWS Academy account
OR
Personal AWS account
STEP 2 — Open S3 Service

After login:

TOP SEARCH BAR

At top of AWS page you will see:

🔍 Search services

Type:

S3

Click:

S3

You will now enter S3 Dashboard.

STEP 3 — Create Bucket
Find Orange Button

Top right:
🟧 Create bucket

Click it.

STEP 4 — Bucket Configuration

Now you will see:

Create bucket page
1. Bucket Name

Find:

Bucket name

Enter:

my-public-bucket-12345

⚠ Bucket name must be:

unique
lowercase only
no spaces

If error comes:
change number at end.

Example:

my-public-bucket-harsha01
2. AWS Region

Find:

AWS Region

Select nearest region.

Example:

Asia Pacific (Mumbai)
STEP 5 — Disable Public Access

VERY IMPORTANT STEP.

Scroll down slowly.

You will see:

Block Public Access settings

By default all checkboxes are ON.

Uncheck:

☑ Block all public access

When you uncheck:
AWS shows warning.

Tick acknowledgment

☑ I acknowledge that the current settings might result in this bucket and the objects within becoming public.

STEP 6 — Create Bucket

Scroll completely down.

Click:
🟧 Create bucket

✅ Bucket created.

STEP 7 — Open Bucket

You will now see bucket list.

Click your bucket name.

Example:

my-public-bucket-12345
STEP 8 — Upload File

Inside bucket:
You will see tabs:

Objects
Properties
Permissions
Metrics

Stay in:

Objects tab
Upload File

Click:
🟧 Upload

Add File

Click:
🟦 Add files

Select:

image
pdf
text file

Example:

virat.jpg

After selecting:

Click:
🟧 Upload

Wait few seconds.

✅ File uploaded.

STEP 9 — Enable Bucket Public Permission

Now we make bucket public.

Go to Permissions Tab

Top tabs:
Click:

Permissions
Scroll Down

Find:

Access Control List (ACL)

Click:
🟦 Edit

Public Access

Under:

Everyone (public access)

Tick:
☑ Read

AWS warning appears.

Tick acknowledgment.

Click:
🟧 Save changes

✅ Bucket public permission enabled.

STEP 10 — Enable Object Public Permission

Now object/file itself must become public.

Go to Objects Tab

Click uploaded file name.

Example:

virat.jpg
Open Permissions

Inside object:
Click:

Permissions tab
Edit ACL

Scroll down.

Find:

Access Control List (ACL)

Click:
🟦 Edit

Enable Public Read

Under:

Everyone (public access)

Tick:
☑ Read

Click:
🟧 Save changes

✅ File is now public.

STEP 11 — Test File URL

Inside object page:

Top right:
Copy:

Object URL

Looks like:

https://my-public-bucket.s3.amazonaws.com/virat.jpg
Open Incognito

Open new incognito tab.

Paste URL.

If image/file opens:
✅ Experiment successful.

PART 2 — S3 Versioning

Versioning means:
Old file versions are saved automatically.

STEP 1 — Open Bucket

Go to:

S3
Your bucket
STEP 2 — Properties Tab

Top tabs:
Click:

Properties
STEP 3 — Bucket Versioning

Scroll down.

Find:

Bucket Versioning

Click:
🟦 Edit

STEP 4 — Enable Versioning

Select:
🔘 Enable

Click:
🟧 Save changes

✅ Versioning enabled.

STEP 5 — Test Versioning

Upload:

report.pdf

Again upload:
another file with SAME NAME:

report.pdf

AWS now:

keeps old version
creates new version
STEP 6 — View Versions

Go:
Objects tab

Top area:
☑ Show versions

Enable it.

Now you can see:

old version
new version
STEP 7 — Delete Object

Select object.

Click:
🗑 Delete

Object disappears.

BUT:
older versions still exist.

This is called:

Delete Marker

You can restore file later.

PART 3 — Cross Region Replication (CRR)

Meaning:
Copy files automatically from one region to another region.

Example:

Source → Mumbai
Destination → Ohio
IMPORTANT REQUIREMENTS

Before CRR:
✅ Versioning ON in both buckets
✅ Different regions

STEP 1 — Create Destination Bucket

Create another bucket.

Example:

dest-crr-bucket-123

Choose DIFFERENT region.

Example:

Ohio

Enable versioning there too.

STEP 2 — Open Source Bucket

Open original bucket.

Go to:

Management tab
STEP 3 — Replication Rules

Find:

Replication rules

Click:
🟧 Create replication rule

STEP 4 — Configure Rule

Enter:

academy-crr-rule

Status:
🔘 Enabled

Scope:
🔘 Apply to all objects

STEP 5 — Select Destination Bucket

Choose:
🔘 Another bucket

Select destination bucket.

STEP 6 — IAM Role

Select:
🔘 Use AWS-managed role

STEP 7 — Save Rule

Click:
🟧 Save

✅ Replication active.

STEP 8 — Test CRR

Upload file in source bucket.

Example:

image.png

Now open destination bucket.

If same file appears automatically:
✅ CRR successful.

PART 4 — Static Website Hosting

You can host HTML website using S3.

STEP 1 — Create Files

Create:

index.html

and

error.html
Example index.html
<h1>Hello Harsha</h1>
<h2>My First AWS Website</h2>
Example error.html
<h1>404 Error</h1>
<h2>Page Not Found</h2>

Save both files.

STEP 2 — Create Bucket

Create new bucket.

Example:

my-static-site-bucket

Disable:
❌ Block Public Access

Create bucket.

STEP 3 — Upload HTML Files

Inside bucket:
Objects → Upload

Upload:

index.html
error.html
STEP 4 — Enable Static Website Hosting

Go:

Properties tab

Scroll down.

Find:

Static website hosting

Click:
🟦 Edit

STEP 5 — Configure Hosting

Select:
🔘 Enable

Choose:
🔘 Host a static website

Enter:

Index document:

index.html

Error document:

error.html

Click:
🟧 Save changes

✅ Website hosting enabled.

STEP 6 — Make Website Public

Go:

Permissions

ACL → Edit

Enable:
☑ Everyone → Read

Save.

STEP 7 — Make HTML Files Public

For BOTH files:

open file
Permissions
ACL Edit
Everyone → Read
Save
STEP 8 — Open Website

Go:
Properties → Static website hosting

Copy:

Bucket website endpoint

Looks like:

http://mybucket.s3-website-ap-south-1.amazonaws.com

Paste in browser.

✅ Website opens.

STEP 9 — Test Error Page

Add wrong URL:

http://bucket-url/abc.html

You should see:
✅ error.html page.
